`pygrametl` is a Python framework for building extract-transform-load (ETL) workflows. Its existing parallel implementation uses separate processes to bypass the Global Interpreter Lock (GIL), while row-by-row database operations leave workers waiting for replies. We evaluate whether newer Python concurrency mechanisms and PostgreSQL pipeline mode can reduce these costs. The study compares multiprocessing, asynchronous extraction, free-threaded CPython, and subinterpreters; subinterpreters are assessed with microbenchmarks, while the other approaches are tested on a star-schema workload of up to 4.5 million fact rows. At the largest size, multiprocessing reduces execution time from 113.6 s to 55.5 s, and pipelining reduces it further to 36.8 s sequentially. The fastest configuration combines pipelining with free-threading, completing in 17.5 s, but three of its 120 runs fail with segmentation faults. Subinterpreters offer no clear enough advantage in the measured data-transfer workloads to justify replacing multiprocessing. Overall, database round trips are the most consequential target in this workload; free-threading can add speed, but its reliability needs further investigation.
